<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Guinea pigs need to eat almost constantly; skipping food for several hours is abnormal, not normal "fussiness".</li>
<li>Not eating, fewer or smaller droppings, and a hunched posture together point to GI stasis, which can become life-threatening within a day.</li>
<li>There is no safe home cure for GI stasis. Supportive care at home can help while you get to a vet, but it does not replace veterinary treatment.</li>
<li>Dental problems, pain, stress and diet changes are the most common root causes behind a guinea pig going off its food.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why a Guinea Pig Not Eating Is Always Urgent</h2>
<p>Guinea pigs are hindgut fermenters: a huge part of their digestion happens in a large cecum that depends on a steady supply of hay and fibre moving through it. When a guinea pig stops eating, that movement slows down. Gas and bacteria can build up, the gut can stretch painfully, and appetite drops further, which makes the whole cycle worse. Unlike a cat or dog that might comfortably skip a meal, a guinea pig that goes without food for even half a day is already at risk.</p>
<p>This is why "let's see if she eats later" is the wrong approach. If your guinea pig has not touched hay, pellets or vegetables for several hours, treat it as urgent and start making a same-day or emergency vet appointment.</p>

<h2>Common Causes of Appetite Loss in Guinea Pigs</h2>
<p>Loss of appetite is a symptom, not a diagnosis. The underlying cause can be mild or serious, and often only a vet exam (sometimes with an X-ray) can tell the difference.</p>

<table class="pz-compare-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Possible cause</th><th>Other clues to look for</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dental disease (overgrown or malaligned molars)</td><td>Dropping food, chewing oddly, drooling, weight loss despite trying to eat</td></tr>
<tr><td>GI stasis or bloat</td><td>Fewer or smaller droppings, hunched posture, a tight or swollen belly, teeth grinding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pain anywhere in the body (joints, bladder, etc.)</td><td>Reluctance to move, hiding, a hunched "ball" posture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Urinary problems (bladder stones or infection)</td><td>Straining to urinate, blood in urine, squeaking when handled</td></tr>
<tr><td>Respiratory infection</td><td>Sneezing, discharge from the nose or eyes, noisy breathing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stress (new cage, new cage mate, loud noise, temperature change)</td><td>Appetite usually returns within hours once the stressor is removed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sudden diet change or low-quality hay</td><td>Picking at food, loose stool, no other illness signs</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Because several of these causes overlap in how they look from the outside, do not try to guess the cause and treat it yourself. A vet exam, and sometimes bloodwork or imaging, is the only reliable way to know what is actually going on.</p>

<h2>How to Recognise GI Stasis Early</h2>
<p>GI stasis is the complication that makes "my guinea pig isn't eating" so dangerous. It is a slowing or stopping of normal gut movement, and it can be triggered by almost any of the causes above, or simply by pain and stress on their own. Watch for this combination of signs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No interest in hay, pellets or favourite vegetables</strong> — often the first sign owners notice.</li>
<li><strong>Fewer droppings, smaller droppings, or none at all</strong> — check the cage floor, not just the litter tray.</li>
<li><strong>A hunched, "roached" posture</strong> with the back arched and belly pulled in.</li>
<li><strong>Teeth grinding or soft grinding/clicking sounds</strong>, which can signal abdominal discomfort.</li>
<li><strong>Lethargy</strong> — sitting still in one spot, not responding normally to being picked up.</li>
<li><strong>A firm, bloated, or gassy-feeling abdomen</strong> on gentle touch.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you see two or more of these signs together, do not wait for the next morning. Guinea pigs can decline very quickly once gut motility stops, and the earlier treatment starts, the better the outcome tends to be.</p>

<h2>What to Do Right Now</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Call an exotics-experienced vet immediately.</strong> Not every general veterinary clinic treats guinea pigs regularly, so if you do not already have one lined up, search for an "exotic" or "small mammal" vet near you and explain the symptoms when you call.</li>
<li><strong>Check droppings and the belly while you wait for the call back.</strong> Note how long it has been since you last saw normal droppings and whether the abdomen feels swollen or hard — your vet will want this information.</li>
<li><strong>Keep your guinea pig warm and in a quiet space.</strong> Stress and cold can make things worse. Avoid forcing exercise or handling more than necessary.</li>
<li><strong>Offer favourite foods, but do not force-feed.</strong> Fresh grass, cilantro, or a preferred leafy green placed right in front of them can sometimes tempt a reluctant eater. Do not syringe-feed liquids or critical-care formula unless a vet has shown you how, since doing it incorrectly can cause aspiration.</li>
<li><strong>Do not give human medications, pain relievers, or home remedies</strong> you have found online. Guinea pigs can be extremely sensitive to certain drugs, and the wrong one can be fatal.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/small-pet-health-checker/">Small Pet Health Symptoms Checker</a> can help you organise what you are seeing before the vet visit, and our <a href="/tools/guinea-pig-care-guide/">Guinea Pig Care Guide</a> covers day-to-day diet and housing that help prevent some of these problems in the first place.</p>

<h2>What a Vet Visit Usually Involves</h2>
<p>A vet will typically start with a hands-on exam, feeling the abdomen for gas or firmness, checking body condition and hydration, and looking in the mouth for overgrown or sharp molar points (which often requires sedation to see properly, since guinea pig mouths are small and deep). Depending on findings, they may recommend an X-ray to check for gas buildup or blockage, pain relief, fluids, gut-motility medication, or supportive syringe feeding taught to you under their guidance. Treatment plans vary a lot depending on the underlying cause, which is exactly why a guess-and-wait approach at home is risky.</p>

<h2>How to Lower the Risk Going Forward</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unlimited good-quality grass hay</strong> should make up most of the diet — it is the single biggest factor in healthy gut motility.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a routine.</strong> Feed at consistent times and avoid sudden food changes.</li>
<li><strong>Weigh your guinea pig weekly.</strong> A kitchen scale and a simple log make it much easier to catch a slow decline before it becomes an emergency.</li>
<li><strong>Watch droppings daily</strong> as part of normal cage cleaning — a sudden drop in number or size is often the earliest warning you will get.</li>
<li><strong>Reduce avoidable stress</strong> such as sudden cage changes, predator sounds, or being housed alone if your guinea pig would do better with a bonded companion.</li>
<li><strong>Schedule regular dental checks</strong>, especially for guinea pigs prone to molar problems, since dental pain is one of the most common hidden causes of appetite loss.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on feeding basics, see our <a href="/tools/guinea-pig-food-calculator/">Guinea Pig Food Portion Calculator</a> and <a href="/tools/guinea-pig-vitamin-c-guide/">Guinea Pig Vitamin C guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>How long can a guinea pig go without eating?</h3>
<p>Not long at all. Signs of trouble can start within a matter of hours, and guinea pigs are considered at risk of GI stasis after roughly half a day without food. Treat any guinea pig that has skipped hay, pellets and vegetables as needing same-day veterinary attention.</p>

<h3>Is it normal for a guinea pig to be a picky eater sometimes?</h3>
<p>Guinea pigs can have food preferences and may ignore a new vegetable or a changed pellet brand. But refusing hay as well, or going quiet at every usual feeding time, is not normal pickiness — it is a sign something is wrong and needs a vet check.</p>

<h3>What does guinea pig poop look like when something is wrong?</h3>
<p>Healthy droppings are plentiful, oval, and fairly firm. Warning signs include noticeably fewer droppings than usual, droppings that are smaller or misshapen, strings of droppings connected by fur or mucus, or no droppings at all for several hours.</p>

<h3>Can stress alone make a guinea pig stop eating?</h3>
<p>Yes. A new cage, a new cage mate, a loud noise, or a change in temperature can all cause a temporary drop in appetite. The difficulty is that stress-related appetite loss looks identical at first to the start of GI stasis, so it still needs to be treated as urgent until a vet rules out anything more serious.</p>

<h3>Can I treat GI stasis at home?</h3>
<p>No. There is no safe, reliable home treatment for GI stasis. Supportive steps such as keeping your guinea pig warm, quiet, and offering favourite foods can help while you get to a vet, but the underlying gut slowdown usually needs veterinary pain relief, fluids and guided feeding to resolve safely.</p>

